\chapter{Integer and floating-point semantics}
\label{ch:8}
\index{Integer and floating-point semantics}

On1x exposes checked signed and unsigned operations in its numeric profiles and keeps intentional wrapping explicit. Integer widths matter at both source and foreign boundaries. A value of type \OnxCodeTerm{u64} can represent values beyond the positive range of \OnxCodeTerm{i64}. A numeric conversion tests the value for the destination domain. A bit conversion preserves a representation and can therefore produce a different mathematical interpretation.

\OnxInputCode[]{../../on1x/examples/unsigned_f32.on1x}
\section{What to test at a boundary}
The example creates the all-ones unsigned value from a signed bit pattern and wraps it through zero. It also computes a result in \OnxCodeTerm{f32} and inspects its bits. Add cases for a largest supported integer, a narrowing conversion outside range, signed zero, and subnormal values when applicable. Compare the documented trap or bit result for the same profile. Avoid tolerance comparisons when the goal is to verify a bit conversion or distinguish signed zeros. Foreign \OnxCodeTerm{float} and \OnxCodeTerm{double} signatures add target and representation obligations in \cref{ch:96}.

\section{Pick the question before the conversion}
Suppose a one-byte counter is reading 255. There are at least three
plausible requirements for its next tick. If 256 is a valid measurement,
convert to a wider unsigned type \emph{before} adding. If passing the
limit means the measurement is invalid, use checked addition at the
original width. If it is a circular sequence number, use the wrapping
operation at that width. Widening a checked sum afterwards cannot rescue
an operation that already trapped. For a concrete test, add one byte to
255 in separate runs: expect 256 for the widened calculation, an
arithmetic failure for the checked byte calculation, and zero for the
wrapping byte calculation. Give both operands the width of the operation.

The last bit experiment in the example has a similarly precise reading.
Importing bits equal to one into binary32 constructs the smallest positive
subnormal, not the floating value one. Doubling it moves one step further
along the subnormal scale. Exporting the bits therefore yields the integer
two. Contrast this with converting integer one numerically to binary32
and multiplying by two: that computation produces the floating number
two, whose exponent field is nonzero. A regression that prints just
``doubled successfully'' cannot distinguish the paths. Testing the bit
result checks both the import operation and that the calculation did not
silently flush the subnormal to zero.

